[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
They were merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and the
subject of a date report says only that a date is wrong. Read by tenant, channel and body, they fall
into three problems. Each now has its own ticket.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

| problem | counted in the table as | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | the `parser` row | <a href="{REPO}/1">#1</a> |
| `hint` | the `hint` row | <a href="{REPO}/2">#2</a> |
| one-day move | the note under `parser`: filed there by tenant, a bug of its own | <a href="{REPO}/3">#3</a> |

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 fixes the parser. It does not fix the other two: their reports stay open
after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** A day-first tenant books 2 December
and the invite says 12 February. Every channel, every tenant on file.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** Alder Physio, Copper Studio and
Pale Harbour run booking-v3 in day-first locales. Their people type what the hint tells them, month
first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The result looks like the parser bug, but only through
the form, and the parser fix leaves it in place. The fix is the hint, or reading the field the way
the hint says.

**`one-day`: a date moves by one day, not by a swap.** "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." "The invite arrived before I made the booking." "Every booking in June is one
day off." These are the reports the desk already read as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`). A month
swap never moves a date by one day, so these are their own bug: a time-zone or daylight-saving
shift. The table counts them under `parser`, where they are filed by tenant; they have their own
ticket.

## Why intake got the split wrong

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser: an `en-US` browser is tagged `hint`. The cause
depends on the tenant's locale and form (`causes.ts`), so intake's tags and the table above
disagree. Count by tenant, not by browser.

## Lessons

- A merge on subject line counts symptoms. Different bugs share a symptom, so the count was right
  and the "one issue" was not.
- Before a big ticket goes to known, read the rows that don't fit the pattern. The desk had found
  the one-day moves, and they went into a footnote.

